#{[17592186216653 :answer/value "\\exp (-\\int h(y)dy)"] [17592186216654 :answer/value "\\exp (\\int h(y)dy)"] [17592186217762 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=2xy-x\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$"] [17592186217762 :question/worked-solution "For $P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy=0$, an integrating factor $I(x)$ must satisfy\n$$\nI P_y=I'Q+I Q_x,\\qquad \\frac{I'}I=\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{Q}=g(x).\n$$\nConsequently\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\mathrm dx\\right).\n$$\nFor this equation take $P=x-2xy$, $Q=1$. Then $g(x)=-2x$ and $I(x)=e^{-x^2}$; direct differentiation verifies exactness. The ratio criterion is used where its denominator is nonzero."] [17592186217792 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186391638] [17592186217792 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186391643] [17592186217793 :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x-({x}^{3}+2{y}^{4})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{4}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y,\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant."] [17592186217795 :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$$\n(y+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(y)={e}^{y}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that the function $I(y)={e}^{y}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$"] [17592186217795 :question/worked-solution "Multiply each coefficient of the original equation by $I(y)=e^y$. The equation to test is\n$$\n((y+x)e^y)\\,\\mathrm dx+(xe^y)\\,\\mathrm dy=0.\n$$\nThis is the required transformed equation. In fact,\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}((y+x)e^y)=(1+x+y)e^y,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(xe^y)=e^y.\n$$\nThese are not identically equal, so $e^y$ does **not** make this equation exact. Selecting the product equation alone does not establish that it is an integrating factor."] [17592186217796 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n(7x-4xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(4xy-7x),\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-4}}\n$$"] [17592186217796 :question/worked-solution "For an integrating factor depending only on $x$, exactness requires\n$$\n\\frac{I'}I=\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{Q}=g(x),\\qquad I(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\mathrm dx\\right).\n$$\nHere $P=7x-4xy$ and $Q=1$, so\n$$\nP_y=-4x,\\qquad Q_x=0.\n$$\nThus $g(x)=-4x$ and $I(x)=e^{-2x^2}$. These identify the four requested selections in their existing fields."] [17592186217797 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=-x({y}^{2}+1)\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$"] [17592186217797 :question/worked-solution "For an integrating factor depending only on $y$, exactness requires\n$$\nI P_y+I'P=I Q_x,\\qquad\\frac{I'}I=-\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{P}=-h(y).\n$$\nTherefore the requested selections are\n$$\nh(y)=\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{P},\\qquad I(y)=\\exp\\left(-\\int h(y)\\,\\mathrm dy\\right).\n$$\nHere $P=x(y^2+1)$, $Q=1$, giving $h(y)=2y/(1+y^2)$ where the ratio is defined. Hence $I(y)=1/(1+y^2)$; this factor extends to every real $y$ and makes the equation exact even at $x=0$."] [17592186228039 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186725842] [17592186228039 :answer-field/type #edb/ref 17592186045492] [17592186228039 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186725842] [17592186228039 :answer-field/id #uuid "1d103008-5f2b-56d3-bf46-9acb13be1e14"] [17592186228041 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186725840] [17592186228041 :answer-field/type #edb/ref 17592186045492] [17592186228041 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186725840] [17592186228041 :answer-field/id #uuid "9b0e257f-3041-5c27-9229-515be1b893d3"] [17592186228059 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186725843] [17592186228059 :answer-field/type #edb/ref 17592186045492] [17592186228059 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186725843] [17592186228059 :answer-field/id #uuid "2a0d502e-48ef-5a89-a581-fcf1fd3744f6"] [17592186228080 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186725839]}
